PC Builder Event 
(Part of SHINE 2K25 – Technical Fest Organized by the Department of MCA)  

---



**Objectives of the Event:**  
1. To enhance participants’ knowledge of computer hardware components and configurations.  
2. To develop critical thinking, analytical, and budgeting skills.  
3. To promote teamwork, coordination, and time management.  
4. To encourage practical learning through competition and interaction.  
5. To create awareness about assembling cost-efficient and high-performance systems.

---

**Materials Required:**  
• Computer component cards or virtual drag-and-drop interface.  
• Budget sheets and PC building software.  
• Timer or stopwatch.  
• Score sheets for judges.  
• Display screen or projector (for live demonstration).  

---

**General Rules for Participants:**  
1. Each team must consist of two members.  
2. Use of the internet, mobile phones, or any external devices during the rounds is strictly prohibited.  
3. There will be three rounds in total.  
4. There will be no elimination.  
5. No passing of questions is allowed.  
6. Negative marks will be awarded for incorrect answers.  
7. Participants must follow all instructions given by the organizers.  
8. Judges’ decisions will be final and binding.  

---

**Description of Rounds:**  

**Round 1: Drag & Drop (45 seconds)**  
• A virtual computer image with empty slots will be displayed.  
• Participants must drag and drop each component to its correct position.  
• Among the two team members, one remains on stage while the other operates the system.  
• Each correct placement earns points; incorrect ones carry negative marks.  
• Submit before the timer ends (45 seconds). Auto-submission occurs once time runs out.  

**Round 2: Matching Budget (50 seconds)**  
• Teams will pick a lot and select a PC category.  
• Each team will be given a fixed budget of ₹1,00,000.  
• Participants must select compatible components that fit within the budget.  
• Scoring is based on compatibility, performance, and cost-efficiency.  
• Overspending leads to negative marks.  
• Time limit: 50 seconds.  
• The judge’s decision will be final.  

**Round 3: Troubleshooting (90 seconds)**  
• Each team will pick a lot containing a set of system-related problems.  
• Participants must find the correct answers from the given choices and justify them.  
• Scores are based on accuracy, clarity of explanation, and proper troubleshooting steps.  
• Time limit: 90 seconds.  
• The judge’s decision will be final.  

